## The facts that shaped it, all verified by the runner rather than assumed

- **The cloud accounts hold zero hosts.** So this is not an inventory; it
  is a definition. Meraki is driven by ids, not connected to.
- **Meraki has no check mode** — 0 of 309 network modules. `--check` skips
  every task and reports success. The plan playbook is the only preview,
  and Apply is a hard gate behind a plan somebody has read.
- **The same bug, twice.** Meraki's apply built only the network while its
  plan promised VLANs; Azure's apply built `subnets[0]` while its plan
  compared resource-group existence. Both found by the convergence test —
  plan → apply → re-plan to all-unchanged after adding a site *and* an
  element — which is now the acceptance rule for any kit.
- **Scope is not symmetric.** Meraki's org id is a var; Azure's
  subscription is an env var the collection reads; AWS's region is a var
  over a scheme default. Encoded per provider (`SCOPE_AS_ENV`).
- **AWS forbids security-group names beginning `sg-`** and the module
  swallows the reason. The form refuses it with the real one.
- **ansible-core 2.19**: a `vars:` entry whose default falls back to itself
  is a recursion loop on the *second* run. ShellMate names it
  `plan_job_id` on the way in.

## Decisions Steven took

- ShellMate is the sole git writer; the runner's `runner/project/**` is a
  mirror and is not committed there. (The runner still commits its own
  `api/` and README.)
- The test resources stay: four `deploy-test-*` Meraki networks (VLANs 10
  and 20), `rg-azure-test-001/002`, three AWS VPCs with nine subnets.

## Left unfinished, deliberately

- **`destroy.yml`** is in every kit and wired to nothing. It wants a plan
  of its own and a double confirmation, and Azure's and AWS's are
  *unproven* — proving them deletes what Steven asked to keep. AWS's
  ordering (SGs before the VPC) is reasoned, not tested.
- **Claiming real MX/MS serials** is written and unproven; the org has no
  claimed devices.
- **Scheduled applies** — #611, blocked on an always-on host.
- **Inventory hosts** from `aws_ec2`/`azure_rm` — its own issue if VMs
  ever appear.

## One thing Steven has to do

**Press the three "Commit the … kit" buttons once**, in the Deployments
tab of the ShellMate whose Settings → Ansible point at the runner and at
GitHub. Nothing commits the runner's working tree, so all three kits exist
only on its disk until then. The button fetches them fresh at that moment.

## Recommended starting point for the next run

Teardown. Wire `destroy.yml` as a doubly-confirmed action behind a plan
of its own, and prove Azure's and AWS's against the test resources — the
first time either is run should be by a person watching, against
resources nobody minds losing. That is also the natural moment to decide
what "forget a deployment" should offer.

After that, the team-and-identity cluster (#566, #526, #541) is still the
best-shaped next batch; #566 should build on `setup_bundle.PARTS`.
